The {{nihongo|'''Nankō Port Town Line'''|南港ポートタウン線|Nankō Pōto Taun-sen}}, also known as the '''New Tram''', is an [[automated guideway transit]] (AGT) line operated by [[Okami Metro Co., Ltd.]] in [[Okami]], [[Okaiken]]. It is the only AGT line in the Okami Metro network and carries the line code '''P'''. The line is identified by the color '''light blue''' ({{HTML color|#71a7d8}}). The line runs from {{nihongo|Minato-misaki|港岬}} to {{nihongo|Iyonada|伊予灘}}, spanning {{cvt|8.7|km|mi|0}} and serving 9 stations, all within the Minato ward.

The Nankō Port Town Line was designed to serve Okami's [[port]] and [[industrial]] area, providing a critical transit link for workers in the maritime and logistics sectors. The line runs along the waterfront, connecting the port's southern tip at Minato-misaki with the residential and commercial areas of the Minato ward, terminating at Iyonada in the north. The line's route passes through several key port facilities, including the trade port area of Hokko-machi and the waterfront district of Shio-machi.

The line is technically distinct from the rest of the Okami Metro network. Unlike the heavy rail metro lines, which use [[third rail]] [[direct current]] electrification, the Nankō Port Town Line uses '''600 V [[Three-phase electric power|3-phase]] [[Alternating current|AC]]''' power, supplied via a [[third rail]] system. The line has a maximum speed limit of {{cvt|55|km/h|mph|0}}, significantly lower than the {{cvt|80|km/h|mph|0}} operating speed of the main metro lines. The line uses [[automated guideway transit]] technology, with driverless trains guided by a concrete guideway rather than conventional rails.

The Nankō Port Town Line offers interchanges with four other metro lines and one OR line: the [[Chūō Line (Okami)|Chūō Line]] and [[Tedorigawa Line]] at [[Nagata-cho]] ({{OMSN|P|02}}), the [[Nanboku Line (Okami)|Nanboku Line]] at [[Kitanada]] ({{OMSN|P|05}}), and the [[Miyoshi Line]] and [[OR Minato Line]] at its northern terminus of [[Iyonada]] ({{OMSN|P|09}}). These connections allow port area workers to access the broader metro network and provide a transit option for residents of the Minato ward.

==History==

===Planning and construction===

The Nankō Port Town Line was conceived in the late 1970s as part of a broader initiative to improve transit access to Okami's port and industrial area. The port district, which had grown rapidly during the post-war economic miracle, was home to a large workforce employed in maritime trade, shipbuilding, and logistics. However, the area was poorly served by public transit, with workers relying on buses that were frequently delayed by the heavy truck traffic characteristic of the port area.

The [[Okami Municipal Transportation Bureau]] initially considered extending one of the existing heavy rail metro lines into the port area, but the narrow streets and dense industrial development of the port district made conventional subway construction impractical and prohibitively expensive. Instead, the bureau opted for an [[automated guideway transit]] system, which could be built on elevated guideways above existing streets, minimizing disruption to port operations and reducing construction costs.

The AGT technology was relatively new at the time, and the Nankō Port Town Line was one of the first such systems to be built in Okaiken. The line was designed with a 4-car train configuration, using rubber-tired vehicles guided by a concrete guideway. The choice of 600 V 3-phase AC electrification was dictated by the AGT technology, which required a different power supply system from the heavy rail metro lines.

Construction began in 1978 and proceeded rapidly, as the elevated guideway was less complex to build than a bored tunnel. The line's route was designed to follow the waterfront as closely as possible, with stations placed at key port facilities and residential areas. The guideway was built on elevated structures, typically 5 to 8 meters above street level, allowing port traffic to pass freely beneath.

===Opening and early operation===

The Nankō Port Town Line opened on July 1, 1981, the same day as the first segment of the [[Tedorigawa Line]]. The simultaneous opening of the two lines was part of a coordinated effort to provide comprehensive transit coverage for the port area, with the Tedorigawa Line serving as the heavy rail backbone and the Nankō Port Town Line providing finer-grained access within the port district itself.

The line was an immediate success, carrying over 50,000 passengers daily within its first year. The driverless operation was a novelty at the time and attracted significant public attention. The line's automated system proved reliable, and the AGT technology was well-suited to the port area's flat terrain and relatively short distances between stations.

===Modernization===

In 2018, as part of the privatization of the [[Okami Municipal Transportation Bureau]] and the broader modernization of the Okami Metro network, the Nankō Port Town Line's 100 series rolling stock was comprehensively refurbished. The refurbishment included interior upgrades, improved accessibility features, and updated automated control systems. The 100 series, which had been in service since the line's opening in 1981, received new interiors, LED lighting, and improved passenger information displays.

The line's automated control system was also upgraded in 2018, incorporating modern signaling technology and improved safety features. The upgrade allowed for more precise train control and slightly increased service frequency during peak hours. Despite these improvements, the line's maximum speed remains limited to {{cvt|55|km/h|mph|0}}, a constraint of the AGT technology and the line's alignment through the densely developed port area.

