==Rolling stock==

The Nankō Port Town Line operates '''AGT''' (Automated Guideway Transit) rolling stock, consisting of 4-car trains approximately {{cvt|45|m|ft|0}} in length. The trains are rubber-tired and guided by a concrete guideway, rather than running on conventional steel rails. All trains are fully automated and operate without drivers.

;Current fleet
* {{nihongo|'''100 series'''|100系}} — Introduced in 1981, manufactured for the line's opening. The 100 series is the only rolling stock to have ever operated on the Nankō Port Town Line. The fleet was comprehensively refurbished in 2018 as part of the privatization-era modernization program, receiving new interiors, LED lighting, improved accessibility features, and updated automated control systems. Despite being over 40 years old, the refurbished 100 series remains in good condition and is expected to remain in service for the foreseeable future.

The 100 series is unique in the Okami Metro fleet, as it is the only AGT rolling stock and is not compatible with any other line in the network. The trains are powered by 600 V 3-phase AC electricity supplied via a third rail, and are limited to a maximum speed of {{cvt|55|km/h|mph|0}}.

==Through-service==

The Nankō Port Town Line does not operate through-service with any other line, as its AGT technology is incompatible with the heavy rail metro lines. The line operates as a self-contained system, with all services terminating at either Minato-misaki or Iyonada. Passengers transferring to the main metro network must change trains at one of the three interchange stations: Nagatacho (with the [[Chūō Line (Okami)|Chūō Line]] and [[Tedorigawa Line]]) or Kitanada (with the [[Nanboku Line (Okami)|Nanboku Line]]).

